WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.920
The story of Michael Jordan has been&nbsp;
told and re-told. But the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.940
his signature shoe? Not so much.&nbsp;
Let's unbox the ending of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.980
adaptation of this famous sneaker saga.&nbsp;
Warning: spoilers for "Air," just ahead.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.360
When we meet Sonny Vaccaro,&nbsp;
Nike's intrepid basketball scout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.800
he's presented as a gambler – a man who&nbsp;
uses his knowledge of basketball to win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.820
money to pour into yet another bet.&nbsp;
But the chances he takes are always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:32.400
rooted in his instinctive grasp of the game.
In "Air," Sonny's journey is another gambit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.634
as he uses his sense of the game's cultural&nbsp;
landscape to make a giant bet on Michael Jordan.&nbsp;

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.801
"I'm willing to bet my career on Michael Jordan."
"Oh, come on, man."&nbsp;

00:00:43.327 --> 00:00:48.632
By the end of the film, we know that Sonny's&nbsp;
gamble has paid off. He lands the Jordan deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.632 --> 00:00:53.760
gets Nike CEO Phil Knight to agree to give the&nbsp;
Jordan family a revenue share on every shoe sold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.040
He also wins vindication, having advocated&nbsp;
for what everyone else at Nike thought was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.900
a crazy idea. He saw Jordan as the great&nbsp;
player and icon that he would become,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.640
even if no one seemed to believe him but&nbsp;
himself and Jordan's mother, Deloris.&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.220
This time, though, Sonny doesn't just throw the&nbsp;
payoff into the next gamble. Instead, he takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.960
the lessons he learned from the Jordan deal and&nbsp;
pours them into advocacy for still more players,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.600
eventually becoming a crusader for the rights&nbsp;
of college athletes. Sonny has clearly grown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.120
even if he remains a gambler at heart.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.420
As "Air" begins, Phil Knight is a comfortable,&nbsp;
confident CEO who's built Nike into a major player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:39.060
in the sneaker market, even if the company doesn't&nbsp;
quite compete with Converse or Adidas. Nike's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:44.100
running shoe company, Phil is a runner, and these&nbsp;
things all sit well with him. They feel like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.960
reliable standard of business at a time when the&nbsp;
company is facing losses, which makes him very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.300
risk-averse when Sonny suggests betting a huge&nbsp;
chunk of his basketball budget on one player.&nbsp;

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:56.748
"I don't know."

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:57.751
"Seriously?" 

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.000
But as Sonny points out to Phil -- more than&nbsp;
once -- Nike was built on risk and Phil's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.440
willingness to make gutsy choices on his way to&nbsp;
the top. It's only by remembering that feeling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.600
through the rules he's enshrined on his office&nbsp;
wall, that Phil can start to grow and evolve Nike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.280
once again. When he focuses on his breathing,&nbsp;
then makes the call to allow Jordan to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.260
a revenue share on his signature shoe, it's a&nbsp;
sign that he's seen the truth of what the path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:28.080
forward should be. It may seem like a nonchalant&nbsp;
decision, but it's actually a paradigm shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.420
for Nike's zen master.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.860
Though Michael Jordan is the focus of every&nbsp;
other character in "Air," he doesn't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.500
much screen time at all. Instead, the avatar for&nbsp;
the Jordan family is Michael's mother, Deloris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:46.200
who runs the family from her home in Wilmington&nbsp;
and sees Sonny as an earnest dealmaker who could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.240
truly do something special for her son. But&nbsp;
Deloris Jordan is not just a savvy dealmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.560
who understands how her son thinks and&nbsp;
feels. In a movie full of strategists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.900
she proves to be the greatest long-term thinker.
It's Deloris who advocates not just for Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.820
to get a lucrative shoe deal (and a new car),&nbsp;
but for Michael to work with a company that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.560
going to elevate him to the level she feels&nbsp;
he deserves. It's Deloris who tells Sonny what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.720
Michael will do once he makes it to the NBA, and&nbsp;
it's Deloris who realizes that Michael has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.700
potential to be more than just a famous athlete.&nbsp;
She advocates for him through the revolutionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:26.100
idea of revenue sharing, which brought&nbsp;
untold fortune to the Jordan family. Deloris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:31.260
who went on to run charities in her family's name,&nbsp;
saw Michael's rise as something that could lift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:36.352
other Black families alongside her own - and&nbsp;
through her advocacy, she made that possible.&nbsp;

00:03:37.147 --> 00:03:41.040
Rob Strasser, Nike's long-suffering&nbsp;
basketball marketing executive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.680
spends much of "Air" trying to manage everyone's&nbsp;
expectations. He enters the movie intending to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:52.080
pursue multiple middle-of-the-road incoming NBA&nbsp;
players, trying to get the most bang out of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:57.420
division's meager budget. When Sonny comes to&nbsp;
him with the idea of pursuing a single athlete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.860
he balks, insisting that it's the kind&nbsp;
of decision that could lead to all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.601
Nike basketball employees losing their jobs.

00:04:04.778 --> 00:04:06.750
"Look, if anybody back there asks where I am, 

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.660
just tell them I'm sick."
"You got it – sick in the head."&nbsp;

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:15.420
But there's more to this than pride for Rob. As&nbsp;
he explains to Sonny, he's a divorced man who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:20.280
only gets to see his daughter once a week, and his&nbsp;
ability to give her free shoes and prove his worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.440
as a guy who works at Nike is a cornerstone&nbsp;
of that relationship. If he loses his job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.600
he won't just lose income and purpose, but&nbsp;
what he perceives as the best way to forge a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.880
stronger bond in his fractured family. When the&nbsp;
deal works, Rob's trust in Sonny is validated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:41.580
but so is the strength of his convictions. It's a&nbsp;
moment of triumph that hits Rob on several levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.680
which makes it more powerful.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.300
Early in "Air," as Sonny is pleading with Phil&nbsp;
for more resources to devote to the basketball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:53.280
division, Phil begins to expound on why he&nbsp;
likes the company the way it is -- before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.600
the Jordan deal changes everything.&nbsp;
They sell running shoes, Phil argues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.860
which are shoes that people wear while they're&nbsp;
running, but also while they're doing errands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.300
or going to work, or just hanging around&nbsp;
the house. It's a sound business model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:11.820
and while it's not built on cultural relevance,&nbsp;
it is built on serving a very solid need.&nbsp;

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:18.060
When it comes to basketball shoes, Phil estimates&nbsp;
the market cap on the product is perhaps a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.740
units, spread across all companies, because&nbsp;
he just doesn't see people buying basketball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:28.500
shoes to wear out in the world. Signing Jordan,&nbsp;
and building not just one shoe but an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:34.680
line around the young star, changes that forever.&nbsp;
Adidas were the "cool" shoes when the film began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:39.780
but with Jordan at Nike, that began to&nbsp;
shift, and soon Nike was at the center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:45.420
not just of basketball, but pop culture.&nbsp;
The company changed in a matter of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.300
and it would never be the same again.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.700
Though Michael Jordan is a character in "Air,"&nbsp;
and appears on-screen in certain pivotal scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:59.820
we never see the young star's face, except in&nbsp;
archival footage that showcases the real Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:04.440
Jordan. According to director Ben Affleck&nbsp;
in an interview with the Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:08.160
this was a deliberate choice, because&nbsp;
he knew the audience would project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.780
their own version of Michael Jordan onto the&nbsp;
character, no matter how he was portrayed.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:18.660
Despite Jordan's relative absence from the story,&nbsp;
"Air" is all about Jordan, and the recognition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:25.140
his legend even from an early age. In one pivotal&nbsp;
scene, Sonny rewatches a single play from Jordan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:30.420
college days over and over. He realizes that&nbsp;
the play was always designed for the 18-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:36.240
athlete to execute, and despite the pressure,&nbsp;
Jordan was poised to win under any circumstances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.120
It's not just proof of talent or even a cool&nbsp;
head under pressure. For Sonny -- and eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:48.300
for Nike -- it's proof of his absolute greatness,&nbsp;
which would go on to transcend basketball and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:54.180
him an internationally known figure. By the end of&nbsp;
the film, "Air" has made the case, and it's done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.940
despite barely showing Michael on screen.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:01.620
In the final meeting that helps Nike secure&nbsp;
the deal with Michael Jordan, executives set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.780
out to show a video that will hopefully dazzle&nbsp;
their prospective signee, but Sonny interrupts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.860
it. Instead of showing a highlight reel of&nbsp;
Jordan himself and how he could work at Nike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:17.280
Sonny launches into an extended monologue about&nbsp;
Jordan as a player, and his future as a star.&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.560
As Sonny tells Jordan that he's about to&nbsp;
experience the highest highs of any athlete ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.920
as well as the lowest lows, we see a montage of&nbsp;
all of those highs and lows playing out. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:34.380
shows us Jordan's NBA heights, his tabloid&nbsp;
headlines, the death of his father, and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:40.200
his short-lived baseball career before his NBA&nbsp;
comeback. We see all of that while Sonny talks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:45.480
laying out not just Michael's potential, but his&nbsp;
path as a singular figure in American sports.&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.220
It's a vital moment in the film, because&nbsp;
it underscores that we, the audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.480
already know Michael Jordan's future. We've&nbsp;
known it from the beginning. The film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.280
therefore, is not just about a shoe deal.&nbsp;
It's about a consequential moment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.660
took shape at a key point in time, and&nbsp;
changed the world in demonstrable ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.340
that we can still see.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:10.380
The NBA had stars before Michael Jordan,&nbsp;
of course. But "Air" points out that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:15.360
through his early merchandising moves, Jordan&nbsp;
had the potential to become a bigger star than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.500
anyone before him, and in the process&nbsp;
change the face of the NBA forever.&nbsp;

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.960
By building a line centered on Jordan, complete&nbsp;
with commercials and even a logo that reflected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.840
his high-flying likeness, Nike put a spotlight&nbsp;
on an NBA star in ways no other shoe company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:36.660
ever had. He became the centerpiece of an entire&nbsp;
cultural moment, which he was able to back up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:42.180
with major achievements on the basketball court.&nbsp;
Because of that, the popularity of the NBA rose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:47.520
but something else also happened. The nature&nbsp;
of Jordan's deal -- and the revenue stream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:53.700
it created -- meant that future superstars could&nbsp;
follow in his footsteps, making the NBA a league&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.700
of players more than a league of teams.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.420
Before setting out to make "Air," Ben&nbsp;
Affleck reached out to Michael Jordan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:05.460
himself to ask for guidance. It was important&nbsp;
to Affleck, he told The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.480
that the film be something that&nbsp;
Jordan wouldn't object to, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.640
wanted the legend's advice on how to proceed.
According to Affleck, Jordan emphasized the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.140
importance of athlete relations representative&nbsp;
Howard White in making the deal at Nike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.860
giving Chris Tucker more leeway to&nbsp;
emphasize the character in the film.&nbsp;

00:09:23.207 --> 00:09:28.247
"It don't matter about what Phil is&nbsp;
thinking, or anybody is thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:34.560
All that matters is, how much do you believe?"
Most importantly, Jordan emphasized just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.960
crucial his mother was in the process, and even&nbsp;
requested that Viola Davis be the one to play her.&nbsp;

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:43.380
At the time, Deloris was not&nbsp;
a major player in the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:48.720
which left Affleck with an important decision.&nbsp;
The director decided to reframe the narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:54.300
making "Air" much more about the Jordan family&nbsp;
and Deloris in particular, securing Davis for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.040
the role as Jordan requested. It was a decision&nbsp;
that changed the entire shape of the narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.620
particularly the ending, as Deloris becomes the&nbsp;
one who advocates for her son's unprecedented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:10.380
deal. Without that input from Jordan, "Air" would&nbsp;
not have been the same movie it turned out to be.

